    {'para': '**93.** The ending of the 3rd plur. is properly ⟨anti⟩; it suffers abbreviation, however, by the loss of its ⟨a⟩, in verbs whose stem ends in ⟨a⟩.'},
    {'para': '**94.** As a heavy syllable ending in a consonant cannot be gunated, a root like ⟨jīv⟩ makes its 3rd sing. ⟨jīvati⟩; ⟨nind⟩ makes ⟨nindati⟩, etc. See § 53.'},
    {'para': '**95. Euphonic rule.** At the end of a word standing in the final position of a sentence, or alone, ⟨s⟩ and ⟨r⟩ always become *visarga* (*ḥ*); and generally also before ⟨k⟩, ⟨kh⟩, ⟨p⟩, ⟨ph⟩, and before sibilants [⟨ś⟩, ⟨ṣ⟩, ⟨s⟩], whether these stand in the same word, or as initial in the following word; e.g. ⟨vadatas punar⟩ becomes always ⟨vadataḥ punaḥ⟩.'},
    {'para': '**96. Force of the present.** The present indicative signifies 1. Present time. 2. Immediate futurity. 3. Past time, in lively narration (“historical present”).'},
